# 4. Mandatory SQL-to-Prisma Comparison Format

Every major SQL operation must have an equivalent Prisma example wherever one exists.

Use this consistent format:

**Operation: Retrieve users with published posts**

SQL:

```
SELECT u.id, u.name, p.title
FROM "User" u
INNER JOIN "Post" p
ON u.id = p."authorId"
WHERE p.published = TRUE
ORDER BY u.name ASC;
```

Prisma TypeScript:

```
const users = await prisma.user.findMany({
  where: {
    posts: {
      some: {
        published: true,
      },
    },
  },
  select: {
    id: true,
    name: true,
    posts: {
      where: {
        published: true,
      },
      select: {
        title: true,
      },
    },
  },
  orderBy: {
    name: 'asc',
  },
});
```

Explanation:

- SQL INNER JOIN combines related rows.
    
- Prisma expresses the relationship using nested filters and selection.
    
- The returned object structure differs from flat SQL rows.
    

Follow this format throughout the reference.

Do not just give a Prisma schema when a Prisma Client query is expected.

Likewise, do not just provide a SQL command when an actual SQL example is needed.

